  "body": "Found by the read-only review of PR #2985 (issue #2901). The PR records the gap in its decision file. This issue tracks it.\n\n## What happens\n\nPR #2985 adds the law `first_traverse_low`: the clearance plane of a milling operation must be above the top of the start stock and of each fixture, because the first traverse starts from an X/Y that is not known.\n\nIn a 3+2 frame the law does not read the box of the machine table (`crates/app/src/operation/first_traverse.rs:89-99`). The table box is a slab about 1,000,000 mm wide. Read along a tilted tool axis, its \"top\" is very large, and each indexed operation was refused. The PR thus skips the table when the frame is tilted. An upright frame still reads the table. The stock and the fixtures are read in each frame.\n\nWhat stays without a check: the first traverse of each 3+2 operation against the machine table. A tilted clearance plane goes down toward the table on its low side, about 0.36 mm for each mm of travel at B 20 degrees. A real table can thus stand above the plane inside the travel of the machine. That is a collision that passes. Thus the priority is `P1`.\n\n## Possible repair\n\nClip the table box to the travel of the machine (the machine file has the axis limits) before the corner reading. Then the law can read the table in a tilted frame.\n\n## Done when\n\n- A 3+2 operation whose clearance plane goes under the table top inside the machine travel is refused by name.\n- The indexed example operations that post today still post.\n- A machine file with no axis limits gives a defined result (a refusal or a note), not a silent pass.\n\nRefs #2901, #2965\n",
